## Why This Is Asked

This question tests triage of a mixed HTTP, MongoDB, Vault path and teaches safe caching of per-request secrets, a common beginner pitfall.

## Key Concepts

- Latency breakdown and instrumentation
- Vault per-request secret calls vs caching
- MongoDB indexing and query patterns
- Safe TTL-based cache and rotation

## Code Example

```javascript
class VaultCache {
  constructor(fetchFn, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.fetchFn = fetchFn;
    this.ttl = ttlMs;
    this.cache = new Map();
  }
  async get(key) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const cached = this.cache.get(key);
    if (cached && now - cached.ts < this.ttl) return cached.value;
    const value = await this.fetchFn(key);
    this.cache.set(key, { value, ts: now });
    return value;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cache invalidation after Vault rotation?
- What metrics would you monitor to ensure no stale secrets are used during load?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess practical rollout strategy for a new ingestion feature that touches HTTP intake, streaming, and external dependencies. Tests ability to design safe migrations, handle partial failures, and observe impact under load.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and canary rollout
- Idempotent enrichment and deduplication
- Backward/forward compatibility of schemas
- Backfill and migration strategy
- External service reliability (circuit breakers, timeouts) and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo enrichment wrapper
async function enrich(event) {
  if (!config.enrichmentEnabled) return event;
  // dedupe by id and version
  const key = `${event.id}:${event.enrichmentVersion||1}`
  if (cache.dedup.get(key)) return event;
  const enriched = await mlEnrich(event);
  // publish to enriched topic
  kafka.produce('events.enriched', enriched)
  cache.dedup.set(key, true)
  return enriched;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the canary rollout under peak load and roll back safely?
- How would you ensure exactly-once semantics across HTTP, Kafka, and the enrichment path while keeping backward compatibility?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes your ability to diagnose multi-service latency issues under multi-tenant load, with realistic trade-offs between per-tenant isolation, backpressure, and end-to-end tracing.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing and context propagation across HTTP, Redis, DB, Kafka, and sink
- Per-tenant rate budgets and backpressure
- Tail latency analysis and query plan instrumentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: OpenTelemetry span around HTTP handler and downstream calls
const span = tracer.startSpan("http.request", { parent: ctx });
try {
  // propagate span context to Redis and Kafka clients
  await redisClient.call("LIMIT", { tenantId: tenant }, { span });
  await kafkaProducer.send({ key: tenant, value: payload, span });
} finally {
  span.end();
}
```

